          %h2#variants Variants
          %h3#minimal_kan_fibration Minimal Kan fibration
          %p
            A Kan fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : E \to B
            is called a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Kan+fibration"} minimal Kan fibration
            if for all cells
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y : \Delta[n] \to E
            the condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(x) = p(y)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi i
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_i x = \partial_i y
            implies for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi k
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi k
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial_k x = \partial_k y
          %h3#quasifibration Quasi-fibration
          %p
            A
            %strong quasi-fibration
            or
            %strong weak Kan fibration
            or
            %strong inner Kan fibration
            of simplicial sets is defined as above, but with the lifting property only imposed in
            = succeed ":" do
              %em inner horns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi k
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n_k
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi k
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt k \lt n
            %em outer horns
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Λ
                    %mn 0
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n_0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mi n
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n_n
          %p
            This weakened condition then says that
            %em composition
            of cells may be lifted through the quasi-fibration, but not necessarily
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inversion
            of 1-cells. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations+of+quasi-categories"} fibrations of quasi-categories
            for more details.
          %h3#left_and_right_kan_fibration Left and right Kan fibration
          %p
            Similarly, a
            %strong left Kan fibration
            is one that has the lifting property for all horns except possibly the last one. and a
            %strong right Kan fibration
            is one that has the lifting property for all horns except possibly the first one. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations+of+quasi-categories"} fibrations of quasi-categories
            for more details.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
          #EmptyBundleIsKanFibration.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundle"} empty bundle
                is
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundle"} empty bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∅
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \to X
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+squares"} commuting squares
              that one would have to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+property"} lift in
              actually exist
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi Λ
                              %mi k
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo ¬
                                %mo ∃
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∅
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Δ
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Lambda_k^n &amp;\overset{ \not \exists }{\longrightarrow}&amp; \varnothing
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\Delta^n &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; B
                    }
            %p
              (keeping in mind that the
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "horn#ZeroSimplexHasNoHorns"} 0-simplex has no horns
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
              %em no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              from any horn to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+simplicial+set"} empty simplicial set
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict initial object
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#SurjectiveKanFibrations Surjective Kan fibrations
          %p
            Notice that a Kan fibration need
            %em not
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            in any sense:
          #KanFibrationNeedNotBeSurjective.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \,\in\, sSet
              any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
              simplicial set (which is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
              in each degree) is a Kan fibration (the
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundle"} empty bundle
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∅
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi KanFib
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\varnothing \underset{\in KanFib}{\longrightarrow} X
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is due to the fact that
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "horn#ZeroSimplexHasNoHorns"} the 0-simplex has no horns
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horns"} horns
              appearing in the Kan condition
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HornFiller"} (1)
              are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing
              (which is a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict initial object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+bundle"} empty bundle
              is vacuous and hence satisfied.
          %p
            But:
          #KanFibrationWhichIsEpiInDegreeZeroIsEpi.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              As soon as a Kan fibration is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              in degree 0, then it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              in all degrees (and
              %a{:href => "SimpSet#EpimorphismsOfSimplicialSetsDetectedDegreewise"} hence
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              of simplicial sets):
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi KanFib
                      %mi f
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext and
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext is surjective
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ∀
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext is surjective
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \overset{f}{\underset{\in KanFib}{\longrightarrow}} Y
                    \\;\;\text{and}\;\;
                    f_0 \; \text{is surjective}
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\Rightarrow
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\underset{n \in \mathbb{N}}{\forall}
                    \\;\;
                    f_n \; \text{is surjective}
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi n
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[n] \xrightarrow{\sigma_n} Y
              be any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-simplex"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-simplex
              (seen, under the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-simplex"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  \-simplex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lift"} lifts
              through
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of this form:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mover
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi σ
                                        %mi n
                                    %mo ^
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi σ
                                  %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \&amp;
                    \\mathllap{{}^{\widehat{\sigma_n}}}\nearrow
                    \&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow \mathrlap{ {}^{f} }
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n]
                    \&amp;\underset{\sigma_n}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    Y
                    \\mathrlap{\,.}
                    }
            %p
              Now, by the assumption that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_0
              is surjective, we do have such a lift at least for any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex"} vertex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of this form:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mover
                                %mi v
                                %mo ^
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi σ
                                  %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\Delta[0]
                    \&amp;\overset{\widehat{v}}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    X
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow\mathrlap{{}^{f}}
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n]
                    \&amp;\underset{\sigma_n}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    Y
                    \\mathrlap{\,.}
                    }
            %p
              But the left morphism is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+cofibration"} acyclic cofibration
              in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
              against Kan fibrations, meaning that this last square has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lift"} lift
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi n
                        %mo ^
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\sigma_n}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_n
          %p
            In fact:
          #KanFibrationWhichIsEpiOnComponentsIsEpi.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              As soon as a Kan fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↠
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(f) \colon \pi_0(X_\bullet) \twoheadrightarrow \pi_0(Y_\bullet)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              in all degrees (and
              %a{:href => "SimpSet#EpimorphismsOfSimplicialSetsDetectedDegreewise"} hence
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              of simplicial sets):
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              By definition (of
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+groups"} simplicial homotopy groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
              in a connected component are connected by some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zag"} zig-zag
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[0] \hookrightarrow Z
              into (the abstract shape of) such a zig-zag is evidently an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+cofibration"} acyclic cofibration
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+lifting+property"} left lifting property
              against the given Kan fibration (by the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KanFibrationWhichIsEpiInDegreeZeroIsEpi"}
          %p
            In summary:
          #EveryPiZeroEpiIsResolvedByASurjectiveKanFibration.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \xrightarrow{f} Y
              be any morphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              which is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              on
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↠
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(f) \,\colon\, \pi_0(X) \twoheadrightarrow \pi_0(Y)
              (hence presenting an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+epimorphism+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} effective epimorphism
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  \-groupoids
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "effective+epimorphism+in+an+infinity1-category#EffectiveEpisOfInfinityGroupoids"} this Prop.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              factors as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+weak+equivalence"} simplicial weak equivalence
              followed by a Kan fibration which is a degreewise
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
              (and
              %a{:href => "SimpSet#EpimorphismsOfSimplicialSetsDetectedDegreewise"} hence
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
              of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The factorization follows by standard constructions such as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+lemma"} factorization lemma
              or the existence of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KanFibrationWhichIsEpiOnComponentsIsEpi"}
          %p
          #SurjectivityOfAcyclicKanFibrations.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The assumption in Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EveryPiZeroEpiIsResolvedByASurjectiveKanFibration"}
              is met in particular for
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+Kan+fibrations"} acyclic Kan fibrations
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "acyclic+Kan+fibration#AcyclicKanFibrationsAreSurjective"} there
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#acyclic_kan_fibrations_and_weak_homotopy_equivalences Acyclic Kan fibrations and weak homotopy equivalences
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+Kan+fibrations"} acyclic Kan fibrations
              morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              of Kan complexes that are both Kan fibrations as well as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} weak equivalences
              in that they induce isomorphisms on all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+groups"} simplicial homotopy groups
              (i.e. the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+fibrations"} acyclic fibrations
              of Kan complexes) are precisely the morphisms that have the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} right lifting property
              with respect to all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+of+a+simplex"} boundary inclusions
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Delta^n \hookrightarrow \Delta^n
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∂
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mo ∃
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\partial \Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;{}^\exists\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^f
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; Y
                    }
                    \\,.
          %p
            e.g. (
            %a{:href => "#GoerssJardine96"}> Goerss-Jardine, chapter I
            )
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Kan fibrations and acyclic Kan fibrations are both stable under
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Because every class of morphisms defined by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} right lifting property
              is stable under pullback.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              From this it follows readily that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
              form a Brownian
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
          #AcyclicFibrationsDetectedFiberwise.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A Kan fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to Y
              is acyclic precisely if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(y)
              over each vertex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
          %p
            Purely combinatorial proofs of this statement include (
            %a{:href => "#Joyal08"}> Joyal 2008, prop. 8.23
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#RiehlVerity13"} Riehl-Verity 13, lemma 5.4.16 in v2 on arxiv
          %h3#pullback_and_homotopy_pullback Pullback and homotopy pullback
          #PullbackOfKanFibrationSendsLeftHomotopyToFiberwiseHomotopyequivalence.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon X \longrightarrow Y
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KanFibration"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1,f_2 \colon A \longrightarrow X
              be two morphisms. If there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+homotopy"} left homotopy
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 \rightarrow f_2
              between these maps, then there is a fiberwise
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              fibrations
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi f
                        %mn 1
                        %mo *
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≃
                      %msubsup
                        %mi f
                        %mn 2
                        %mo *
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1^\ast X \simeq f_2^\ast X
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GoerssJardine96"} Goerss-Jardine 96, chapter I, lemma 10.6
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
          %h3#on_nerves_of_groupoids On nerves of groupoids
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C \to D
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-surjective+functor"} k-surjective
              for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              and hence a surjective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              precisely if under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(F) : N(C) \to N(D)
              it induces an acyclic fibration of Kan complexes;
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              We know that both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(C)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(D)
              are Kan complexes. By the above theorem it suffices to show that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(f)
              being a surjective equivalence is the same as having all lifts
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi δ
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mo ∃
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %mi N
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi D
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\delta \Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; N(C)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;{}^\exists\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^{N(F)}
                    \\\
                    \\Delta[n] &amp;\to&amp; N(D)
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              We check successively what this means for increasing
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n= 0
                  \. In degree 0 the boundary inclusion is that of the empty set into the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo ↪
                          %mo *
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \hookrightarrow {*}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(D)
                  lifts through
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(F)
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi ∅
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                              %mtd
                                %mi N
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi C
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mo ∃
                                %mo ↗
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi N
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi F
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo *
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                              %mtd
                                %mi N
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi D
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇔
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi N
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi C
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mo ∃
                                %mo ↗
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi N
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                    %mi F
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mo *
                              %mtd
                                %mo →
                              %mtd
                                %mi N
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi D
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \\emptyset &amp;\to&amp; N(C)
                        \\\
                        \\downarrow &amp;{}^\exists\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^{N(F)}
                        \\\
                        {*} &amp;\to&amp; N(D)
                        }
                        \\Leftrightarrow
                        \\array{
                        \&amp;&amp; N(C)
                        \\\
                        \&amp;{}^\exists\nearrow&amp; \downarrow^{N(F)}
                        \\\
                        {*} &amp;\to&amp; N(D)
                        }
                        \\,.
                %p
                  This precisely says that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(F)
                  is surjective on 0-cells and hence that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is surjective on objects.
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
                  \. In degree 1 the boundary inclusion is that of a pair of points as the endpoints of the interval
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo ,
                          %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mo ↪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mo ∘
                          %mo →
                          %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\circ, \bullet\} \hookrightarrow \{\circ \to \bullet\}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Obj
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in Obj(C)
                  all elements in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(F(a),F(b))
                  are hit. Hence that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full functor
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
                  \. In degree 2 the boundary inclusion is that of the triangle as the boundary of a filled triangle. It is sufficient to restrict attention to the case that the map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Delta[2] \to N(C)
                  sends the top left edge of the triangle to an identity. Then the lifting property here evidently is equivalent to saying that for all objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Obj
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b \in Obj(C)
                  the map
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                        %mo :
                        %mi Hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{a,b} : Hom(a,b) \to Hom(F(a),F(b))
                  is injective. Hence that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful functor
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mrow
                            %mtable
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mi a
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mrow
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi Id
                                        %mi a
                                  %mo ↗
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mo ↘
                                    %mi f
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mi a
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mover
                                    %mo →
                                    %mi g
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mi b
                          %mo )
                        %mover
                          %mo ↦
                          %mrow
                            %mi N
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mrow
                            %mtable
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mi a
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mrow
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi Id
                                        %mi a
                                  %mo ↗
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mo ⇓
                                    %mo =
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mo ↘
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi F
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi f
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %mi a
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mover
                                    %mo →
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi F
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mi g
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %mi b
                          %mo )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\left(
                        \\array{
                        \&amp;&amp; a
                        \\\
                        \&amp; {}^{Id_a}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{f}
                        \\\
                        a &amp;&amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp;&amp; b
                        }
                        \\right)
                        \\stackrel{N(F)}{\mapsto}
                        \\left(
                        \\array{
                        \&amp;&amp; a
                        \\\
                        \&amp; {}^{Id_a}\nearrow &amp;\Downarrow^=&amp; \searrow^{F(f)}
                        \\\
                        a &amp;&amp;\stackrel{F(g)}{\to}&amp;&amp; b
                        }
                        \\right)
          %h3#universal_kan_fibration Universal Kan fibration
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+Kan+fibration"} universal Kan fibration
          %h2#relation_to_other_concepts Relation to other concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Kan fibrations and quasi-fibrations are fibrations in two common
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structures on simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                Recall that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^k[n]
                is the boundary of the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n
                with one face removed. If in the above definition one replaces horns with the full boundaries of simplices, one obtaines the definition of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercover"} hypercover
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                A simplicial set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                for which the unique morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi pt
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to pt
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal simplicial set
                is a Kan fibration is called a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            %li
              %p
                A simplicial set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                for which the unique morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi pt
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to pt
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal simplicial set
                is a quasi-fibration/weak Kan fibration is called a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-category
            %li
              %p
                Just as the underlying simplicial set of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
                (see algorithm at
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : G\to H
                of simplicial groups for which in each dimension,
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_n : G_n \to H_n
                is an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            %li
              %p
                A morphism of simplicial sets that has the left
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+property"} lifting property
                with respect to all Kan fibrations is called an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anodyne+morphism"} anodyne morphism
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Kan fibration
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anodyne+morphism"} anodyne morphism
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+Kan+fibration"} acyclic Kan fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right%2Fleft+Kan+fibration"}> right/left Kan fibration
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right%2Fleft+anodyne+map"} right/left anodyne map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+fibration"} inner fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+fibration"} homotopy Kan fibration
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original definition is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"} Daniel M. Kan
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A combinatorial definition of homotopy groups
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/1970006"} doi
          %p
            This was extended to arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
            via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            in Definition 3.2 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On c.s.s. categories
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://dmitripavlov.org/scans/kan-on-css-categories.pdf"} PDF
          %p A standard textbook account is
          %ul
            %li#GoerssJardine96
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Goerss"}> Paul Goerss
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rick+Jardine"} Rick Jardine
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simplicial+homotopy+theory"} Simplicial homotopy theory
          %p
            That
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+realization"} topological realization
            takes Kan fibrations to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibrations"} Serre fibrations
            is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Quillen68
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"}> Daniel Quillen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The geometric realization of a Kan fibration is a Serre fibration
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1968-019-06/S0002-9939-1968-0238322-1"} doi:1968-019-06/S0002-9939-1968-0238322-1
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/proc/1968-019-06/S0002-9939-1968-0238322-1/S0002-9939-1968-0238322-1.pdf"} pdf
          %p reviewed in
          %ul
            %li#GoerssJardine99
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Goerss"}> Paul Goerss
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+F.+Jardine"} J. F. Jardine
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simplicial+homotopy+theory"} Simplicial homotopy theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-0346-0189-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-0346-0189-4
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://web.archive.org/web/19990208220238/http://www.math.uwo.ca/~jardine/papers/simp-sets/"} webpage
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li#Joyal08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Theory+of+Quasi-Categories+and+its+Applications"} The Theory of Quasi-Categories and its Applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "https://lists.lehigh.edu/pipermail/algtop-l/2007q4/000017.html"} Advanced Course on Simplicial Methods in Higher Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://mat.uab.cat/~kock/crm/hocat/advanced-course/Quadern45-2.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/JoyalTheoryOfQuasiCategories.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#RiehlVerity13
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominic+Verity"} Dominic Verity
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy coherent adjunctions and the formal theory of monads
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1310.8279"} arXiv:1310.8279
          %p
            A structured/constructive analogue suited to purposes like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            with equivalent homotopy theory is the notion of effective Kan fibration from
          %ul
            %li
              Benno van den Berg, Eric Faber,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Effective Kan fibrations in simplicial sets
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.12670"} arXiv:2009.12670
            %li
              Benno van den Berg,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Effective Kan fibrations in simplicial sets
              %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i1TkX_86rLo"} yt
        .revisedby
